---

Wireless networking tools. iwd is the modern replacement for wpa_supplicant
and is recommended for new setups. Both can work standalone or under a
network manager.

@code bash
# ---- iw: low-level wireless configuration ----

iw dev                                    # list wireless interfaces
iw dev wlan0 info                         # interface details
iw dev wlan0 link                         # current connection info
iw dev wlan0 scan                         # scan for networks
iw dev wlan0 scan | grep -E "SSID|signal|freq"   # filtered scan

iw dev wlan0 connect "MyNetwork"          # connect to open network
iw dev wlan0 disconnect                   # disconnect

# interface management
iw phy phy0 info                          # hardware capabilities
iw dev wlan0 set power_save off           # disable power saving
iw dev wlan0 set txpower fixed 1500       # set tx power (mBm)

# monitor mode
iw dev wlan0 set type monitor             # enable (interface must be down)
iw dev wlan0 set type managed             # back to managed mode

@code bash
# ---- iwctl: iwd interactive client ----

# iwd service (OpenRC)
rc-service iwd start
rc-update add iwd default

# interactive mode
iwctl                                     # enter interactive shell

# command-line mode
iwctl station wlan0 scan
iwctl station wlan0 get-networks          # list available networks
iwctl station wlan0 connect "MyNetwork"   # connect (prompts for passphrase)
iwctl station wlan0 connect-hidden "SSID" # connect to hidden network
iwctl station wlan0 disconnect
iwctl station wlan0 show                  # connection status

iwctl known-networks list                 # saved networks
iwctl known-networks "MyNetwork" forget   # remove saved network
iwctl known-networks "MyNetwork" set-property AutoConnect yes

iwctl device list                         # list devices
iwctl adapter list                        # list adapters
iwctl adapter phy0 show                   # adapter info

@text
iwd stores network profiles in /var/lib/iwd/ as .open, .psk, or .8021x
files. The passphrase is stored as a pre-shared key (hashed). You can
manually create profiles there.

@code bash
# Example: /var/lib/iwd/MyNetwork.psk
[Security]
PreSharedKey=hex_encoded_psk_here
Passphrase=plaintext_passphrase_here

[Settings]
AutoConnect=true

@code bash
# ---- wpa_supplicant ----

# generate config
wpa_passphrase "MyNetwork" "passphrase" > /etc/wpa_supplicant/wpa_supplicant-wlan0.conf

# connect manually
wpa_supplicant -B -i wlan0 -c /etc/wpa_supplicant/wpa_supplicant-wlan0.conf
dhcpcd wlan0                              # get IP after association

# OpenRC service
rc-service wpa_supplicant start
rc-update add wpa_supplicant default

# wpa_cli interactive
wpa_cli -i wlan0
> scan
> scan_results
> add_network
> set_network 0 ssid "MyNetwork"
> set_network 0 psk "passphrase"
> enable_network 0
> save_config
> quit

@text
wpa_supplicant config file location: the service looks for
/etc/wpa_supplicant/wpa_supplicant.conf or a per-interface file
wpa_supplicant-<interface>.conf.

@code bash
# Example: /etc/wpa_supplicant/wpa_supplicant.conf
ctrl_interface=/run/wpa_supplicant
update_config=1

network={
    ssid="MyNetwork"
    psk="passphrase"
    priority=5
}

network={
    ssid="OpenNetwork"
    key_mgmt=NONE
}

@code bash
# ---- rfkill: manage wireless kill switches ----

rfkill list                               # show all wireless devices
rfkill list wifi                          # wifi only
rfkill list bluetooth                     # bluetooth only

rfkill block wifi                         # software-block wifi
rfkill unblock wifi                       # unblock wifi
rfkill unblock all                        # unblock everything

@text
If rfkill shows "Hard blocked: yes", the hardware switch or BIOS has
disabled the radio. No software command can override a hard block. Check
the physical switch or BIOS/UEFI settings.

Common troubleshooting:

@code bash
# check if driver is loaded
lspci -k | grep -A3 -i wireless
lsmod | grep -i iwl                       # Intel wireless

# check for firmware issues
dmesg | grep -i firmware
dmesg | grep -i iwl

# restart networking
ip link set wlan0 down
ip link set wlan0 up
